  "slug": "sequences-promoter-38cf",
  "summary": "New work shows pTM/ipLDDT misranks functional designs, while glycan-aware pose metrics and bias-aware scoring benchmarks redefine what counts as a validated model.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is that fold-level confidence is being decoupled from the properties models are actually used to predict. One study finds pTM and ipLDDT fail as selection filters for lanmodulin rare-earth-binding designs, with a function-trained model on measured Tb affinity outperforming structural confidence. CARVE supplies glycan-specific pose-quality criteria (oRMSD, irRMSD, Fnat with DP-scaled thresholds) over 1,974 protein-glycan complexes, and HiFiPL-Bench curates a bias-aware PDB/BindingDB benchmark diagnosing nine protein-ligand scoring functions. In parallel, affinity prediction is being pushed off the structural pipeline entirely: STORM and Savant predict binding from sequence and SMILES without docking, while BioLM-Score fuses frozen protein and molecular language-model embeddings with residue-atom geometric likelihoods. Interface work follows suit, with HandShake performing sequence-only partner-specific interface prediction via ProstT5 and cross-chain attention, and TopoPPI mapping interfaces to low-distortion 2D atlases. What is new is evaluation anchored to measured function and interaction chemistry rather than coordinate agreement.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-20"
}
